# The clause-by-clause conformance corpus for the Classic Refal-5 front end.
#
# Every row binds a clause of the syntax reference to the fixture that exercises
# it: `clause|fixture|mode`, where the mode is `accept` (the compiler must
# accept the fixture) or `reject` (it must refuse it, with a diagnostic).
#
# The reference is the Refal-5 syntax reference, cited section by section:
#   http://www.refal.net/refer_r5.html
#
# Clauses 1.1-1.4 are lexical, 2 is the expression grammar, 3 is the sentence
# and program grammar, and 4 is comments. A clause that states a rule is covered
# in both directions wherever the rule has two: what it admits, and what it
# forbids. The negative half is what makes this a conformance corpus rather than
# a smoke test -- a lexer that accepts everything passes every `accept` row.
#
# `every_reference_clause_has_a_traceable_fixture` reads this file, requires the
# clause set below to match the clauses the reference defines, requires every
# fixture to exist, and runs every row.

# --- 1.1 Special characters: quotes, separators, brackets, variable indicators
#         and the system keywords $ENTRY / $EXTERNAL / $EXTERN / $EXTRN.
1.1|classic-syntax.ref|accept
1.1|hello.ref|accept
1.1|extern-equivalence.ref|accept
1.1|bad-unsupported-directive.ref|reject

# --- 1.2 Symbols: identifiers, macrodigits, reals and keyboard characters.
1.2|classic-syntax.ref|accept

# --- 1.2.1 An identifier is alphanumeric, starts with an uppercase letter, is at
#           most 15 characters, treats `-` and `_` as equivalent, and treats
#           upper and lower case as equivalent.
1.2.1|identifier-equivalence.ref|accept
1.2.1|bad-lowercase-identifier.ref|reject
1.2.1|bad-long-identifier.ref|reject

# --- 1.2.2 Macrodigits are non-negative integers whose largest value is 2^32-1.
1.2.2|metacode-macrodigit.ref|accept
1.2.2|bad-signed-macrodigit.ref|reject

# --- 1.2.3 Real numbers, to the reference's BNF: unsigned, or signed with `-` or
#           `+`, and containing a decimal point, an `E`, or both.
1.2.3|classic-syntax.ref|accept
1.2.3|bad-malformed-real.ref|reject
1.2.3|bad-malformed-exponent.ref|reject

# --- 1.2.4 Character symbols are quoted with `'` or `"`, a string may not cross
#           a line break, and a doubled quote embeds the delimiter.
1.2.4|quote-escape.ref|accept
1.2.4|runtime-character-codes.ref|accept
1.2.4|bad-empty-character-literal.ref|reject

# --- 1.3 A variable is a type indicator, a dot, and an index. The dot may be
#         dropped for a one-character identifier or a single-digit number, and
#         the index is case-insensitive.
1.3|shorthand-variables.ref|accept
1.3|variable-index-equivalence.ref|accept
1.3|bad-missing-variable-name.ref|reject
1.3|bad-variable-kind-conflict.ref|reject

# --- 1.4 Invisible characters are ignored between lexical units and act as
#         separators; `s1s2s3` is `s1 s2 s3`, while `s.1s.2s.3` is an error
#         because the dotted form is not self-delimiting.
1.4|shorthand-variables.ref|accept
1.4|bad-juxtaposed-dotted-variables.ref|reject

# --- 2 Expressions: term ::= symbol | variable | (expression) | <f-name expression>;
#       a pattern contains no call brackets.
2|hello.ref|accept
2|runtime-bracket.ref|accept
2|bad-call-in-pattern.ref|reject
2|bad-unclosed-structural-bracket.ref|reject
2|bad-unclosed-call.ref|reject

# --- 3 Sentences and programs: definitions, external declarations with one or
#       more names, sentence and block separators, conditions, and a
#       sentence-ending block.
3|hello.ref|accept
3|classic-syntax.ref|accept
3|multiple-entry.ref|accept
3|extern.ref|accept
3|extern-equivalence.ref|accept
3|condition.ref|accept
3|block-ending.ref|accept
3|condition-block.ref|accept
3|bad-missing-equals.ref|reject
3|bad-missing-colon.ref|reject
3|bad-top-level-sentence.ref|reject
3|bad-empty-function.ref|reject
3|bad-missing-entry.ref|reject
3|bad-extern-missing-semicolon.ref|reject
3|bad-duplicate-function.ref|reject
3|bad-duplicate-extern.ref|reject
3|bad-unresolved-call.ref|reject
3|bad-unbound-variable.ref|reject
3|bad-condition-unbound-variable.ref|reject
3|bad-unclosed-block.ref|reject

# --- 4 Comments: a line beginning with `*`, and a substring between `/*` and
#       `*/` standing between lexical units.
4|classic-syntax.ref|accept
4|conformance-block-comment.ref|accept
4|bad-unterminated-block-comment.ref|reject
